  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>Red Tractor is a UK farm and food assurance scheme covering food safety, animal welfare and environmental protection</li><li>A large part of an assessment is documentary: the assessor asks to see records, not just the farm</li><li>Records commonly checked include health and safety assessments, machinery checks, medicine records and traceability</li><li>A missing record is a non-conformance even where the practice itself was sound</li><li>Records do not pass the audit on their own; good practice does. But the records are what the assessor asks to see</li></ul></div><h2>What Red Tractor assurance checks</h2><p>Red Tractor is a farm and food assurance scheme. A farm in the scheme is assessed against the standards for its sector, and the logo signals to buyers and retailers that those standards are met. The assessment is carried out by an assessor, usually once a year, and covers the farm itself and the records behind it: how stock is kept, how medicines are used, how chemicals are stored, how safety is managed, and how everything is traced. The standards change over time and differ by sector, so always work from the current Red Tractor standards for your enterprise and treat this page as a general guide.</p><h2>What an assessor typically asks to see</h2><p>The exact list depends on the sector and the current standards, but the recurring themes are consistent.</p><ul><li><strong>Health and safety</strong> - risk assessments for the main hazards, and machinery checks showing equipment is maintained under PUWER</li><li><strong>COSHH and chemicals</strong> - assessments and records for pesticides, sheep dip and veterinary medicines, with correct storage and the right certificates of competence</li><li><strong>Medicine records</strong> - what was administered, to which animals, by whom, the batch, and the withdrawal period observed</li><li><strong>Traceability</strong> - inputs, movements and the records that follow the crop or stock through the farm</li><li><strong>Training and competence</strong> - who is trained or certificated, such as NPTC certificates for pesticide application</li></ul><h2>Why the night-before scramble fails</h2><p>Everyone in farming knows the pattern: the assessment is booked and the evening before is spent hunting for records, filling gaps from memory and printing what should already exist. An assessor can usually tell. A record made at the point of work, with a timestamp and an image, is hard to argue with. A folder filled in the night before is easy to spot and easy to doubt. Moving the record to the moment of the work turns audit day into a walk-through of what already exists.</p><h2>What records can and cannot do</h2><p>A records system does not make the farm compliant, keep the stock well, or store the chemicals safely. The farmer and the workers do that. What the record does is prove it was done, on the day it was done, which is exactly what an assessor is there to verify. It so often falls down not because the practice was poor but because the paperwork drifted. Records are evidence, not a substitute for practice, and they do not replace the assessor's judgement or the scheme's own requirements.</p><h2>What to look for in an assurance records tool</h2><ul><li><strong>Records made on the day:</strong> the check logged when it happens, not reconstructed before the visit</li><li><strong>Date and photo:</strong> each record timestamped with an image, hard to dispute later</li><li><strong>One place to look:</strong> a dashboard the office can open in front of the assessor rather than a folder of loose paper</li><li><strong>No per-seat penalty:</strong> per-seat pricing punishes a farm for seasonal, family and contract labour. Quickler charges per report, unlimited users on every bundle</li><li><strong>Findable evidence:</strong> date, worker, animal or field, and the record itself, for the assessor, an insurer or the HSE</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the records and keeps them findable. It does not make the farm compliant, keep the stock, or replace the scheme's own requirements and the assessor's judgement. It records what was done, with a date and a photo, so the evidence exists on audit day. Always work from the current Red Tractor standards for your sector. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your farm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What records does Red Tractor require?</h3><p>The exact records depend on the sector and the current standards, but they commonly include health and safety risk assessments, machinery checks, COSHH records for pesticides and veterinary medicines, medicine and treatment records with withdrawal periods, traceability of inputs and movements, and evidence of training and competence. Always work from the current Red Tractor standards for your enterprise.</p><h3>How do I prepare for a Red Tractor assessment?</h3><p>The most reliable preparation is to keep the records as you go, so the assessment is a walk-through of what already exists rather than a scramble the night before. Records made on the day, with a date and a photo, stand up better than a folder filled in at the last minute. Check the current standards for your sector so you know what the assessor will ask for.</p><h3>Does a records app make my farm pass the audit?</h3><p>No. Records do not pass the audit on their own; good practice on the farm does. But the records are what the assessor asks to see, and a missing record is a non-conformance even where the practice was sound. A tool like Quickler makes the record easy to capture on the day and easy to produce on audit day. It does not replace the practice or the assessor's judgement.</p><h3>Can I keep assurance records over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's workflows run over the WhatsApp Business API. The worker logs the check in their existing WhatsApp chat with text, a voice note or a photo, and the record generates automatically with a date and image on a dashboard the office can open for the assessor. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the farm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-safety-inspection-app-uk.html\">Farm safety inspection app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-machinery-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Farm machinery inspection checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-risk-assessment-app-uk.html\">Farm risk assessment app</a></li></ul>",
